1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned system that estimates the cost of replicating a study. The cost depends on the study's methods, its sample size, its instruments, its duration, its personnel, and its location. The Replication Cost Estimator records each study's characteristics, computes the estimated cost of replication, and produces a breakdown by category. It is a planning tool: it helps funders, researchers, and institutions decide whether a replication is worth the cost.

It reuses:

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the N Calculator (App 12)

the Pipeline Specification Wizard (App 8)

the Effective-Null Builder (App 11)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Instrument Provenance Registry (App 28)

the Replication Tracker (App 26)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for funders, research groups, meta-scientists, institutional review boards, and anyone who wants to know what a replication would cost.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of replication cost estimates from users.

Store each estimate with the study reference, the method, the cost breakdown, and the provenance.

Record the study characteristics: the design, the sample size, the population, the instruments, the duration, and the location.

Record the cost categories: personnel, equipment, materials, travel, participant compensation, publication, overhead, and other.

Record the cost drivers: what makes the replication expensive.

Record the cost assumptions: what assumptions the estimate rests on.

Record the uncertainty: the range of the estimate, not just the point value.

Record the cost per participant, the cost per site, and the cost per month.

Record the comparison with the original study's cost, if known.

Record the currency and the year, with an adjustment for inflation.

Record the funding sources: what funding would be needed and where it might come from.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by estimate ID, study reference, method, cost range, currency, funder, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the study description, the cost assumptions, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by method, by cost range, by funder, by year, by country.

Support exporting an estimate as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing an estimate from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking an estimate to a Physics Knowledge Library work.

Support linking an estimate to a Replication Tracker record.

Support linking an estimate to an Instrument Provenance Registry record.

Support the cost breakdown view: a visualisation of the cost categories.

Support the cost comparison view: a visualisation that compares two or more estimates for the same study.

Support the cost driver view: a visualisation of what makes the replication expensive.

Support the cost timeline: a visualisation of how the cost has changed over time.

Support the funding gap view: a visualisation of the difference between the estimated cost and the available funding.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical estimate in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 estimates.

Versioned: the system records the STAG schema version, the replication cost schema version, and the system version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

